# The Whispering Woods, north of the village: Pip, the toadstool ring,
# and the great oak that keeps the woods' ember in its roots.

place the woods
  title: The Whispering Woods
  the sky: night
  the mist: thick
  the ground: forest floor, 46 by 58 m
  you come in at 0 23, facing 0

  a great oak at 0 -12
  a toadstool ring at -8 3, unlit
  a wisp cloud called the wisps at -8 3, hidden
  Pip at -6.4 5.6, facing 140

  a way to the village at 0 26, turned 180
  a way to the ford at 15 -22, turned -40

  16 path stones in a line from 0 22 to 0 -6
  10 path stones in a line from 2 -8 to 14 -20
  46 pine trees between -22 -28 and 22 28
  14 tall pines between -22 -28 and 22 28
  6 birches between -18 -20 and 18 20
  18 toadstools between -18 -24 and 18 24
  5 big rocks between -20 -26 and 20 26
  4 fallen logs between -16 -20 and 16 20

  when you first arrive
    say: The pines here are older than the village. Mist hangs between them like wet washing, and somewhere, a small voice is crying.

  when you talk to Pip
    if pip is home
      Pip: You lit our ring! My whole family came back. The Warden says you have a kind light.
    otherwise
      Pip: Go away, big light. No. Wait. Don't go away.
      Pip: I'm Pip. My family lives in the toadstool ring, but the ring went dark and they all blew away into the mist. I can't light it. I'm too small.
      choose
        choice: Let me try. My lantern still has a spark.
          Pip: Really? It's just there. The circle of toadstools.
        choice: I'm sorry, Pip. I have to find an ember.
          Pip: The Warden has one, inside the great oak. But it doesn't like visitors. It doesn't like anybody.

  when you use the toadstool ring
    if pip is home
      say: The toadstools glow softly. Wisps bob between them like dandelion seeds.
    otherwise
      say: You hold your lantern low. The spark leans out of it, as if it wants to jump.
      light the toadstool ring
      say: One by one the toadstools catch: blue, then green, then soft white. And out of the mist come tiny lights, dozens of them.
      show the wisps
      remember pip is home
      add 1 to kindness
      Pip: Mum! Dad! Everyone!
      Pip: Thank you, big light. Listen. The Warden asks everyone the same question. Tell it the truth, even if the truth is that you were wrong.

  when you talk to the great oak
    if the warden gave its ember
      The Warden: Go on, little flame. The woods will remember you.
    otherwise
      say: The bark shifts. Two knots open like sleepy eyes, and a voice like a creaking floor rolls out of the roots.
      The Warden: Another lamp-carrier from the village. Your people took fire from these woods for a thousand years. Then they forgot to say thank you.
      The Warden: Why should the woods give its ember to a village that forgot it?
      choose
        choice: Because we need it. Without fire, the Grey will take everyone.
          The Warden: Need is not a reason. Everything that lives needs. Come back when you have a better answer.
        choice: We did forget. I'm sorry. I'll make sure we remember.
          The Warden: Truth. Rare enough in lamp-carriers. Take it, then, and don't forget again.
          say: Deep in the roots, a coal glows red. It rolls into your palm, warm as a sleeping cat.
          you get the woods ember
          add 1 to embers
          put out the great oak
          remember the warden gave its ember
        choice if pip is home: Pip says you're kinder than you sound.
          The Warden: Ha! That wisp talks too much. But a lamp-carrier who stops for a lost wisp is one I can trust.
          say: Deep in the roots, a coal glows red. It rolls into your palm, warm as a sleeping cat.
          you get the woods ember
          add 1 to embers
          add 1 to kindness
          put out the great oak
          remember the warden gave its ember
        choice: Then I'll take it. I'm not asking.
          add 1 to courage
          The Warden: Brave. And foolish. Try to pull it from my roots and see how far you get.
          say: The roots tighten like fingers. You step back, quickly.

  when you look at the great oak
    if the warden gave its ember
      say: The oak sleeps again. Where the ember was, the roots are curled around nothing, like a hand after letting go.
    otherwise
      say: The oldest tree you have ever seen. Down among its roots something glows red, slow as breathing.

  when you leave for the ford
    if the warden gave its ember
      go to the ford
    otherwise
      say: You could go on to the river. But the oak's ember is right here, glowing in its roots.
      choose
        choice: Go on to the river anyway.
          go to the ford
        choice: Stay a while.
          say: You turn back towards the great oak.
